WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
The first business venture I tried
was selling sweets at school.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.160
At the time I was looking
to buy a PlayStation,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.880
and I wanted to buy it myself,
with my own money.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:08.960
At that age

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.840
people were not allowed
to go outside of schoolgrounds

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:12.880
during the school day,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.640
so I thought "perfect.
I've got a captive customer base."

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.360
My first tip is...

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.600
Eat the frog means start your day
with the hardest task you have.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.040
And it means the rest of your day

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.760
becomes a lot
easier and smoother.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.240
In sixth form you'd have
free periods, for example,

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.560
and there was times where
I would sit in the free period

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:50.960
and do financial forecasts,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.440
people would look over my shoulder
and think I'm insane.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.680
But it was difficult to explain,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.280
but I thought
if I'm prioritising this now

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.680
because I know I've got a due date
for an essay in two weeks,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.560
and it's just about
managing that workflow.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.280
...is my next top tip.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.280
Both my parents are deaf,

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.720
so my older sister and I have been
carers to them our whole lives.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.880
I think we matured a lot earlier
as children because we had to.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.000
If I went all the way back
to the start of Doorsteps,

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:23.080
that starts when I moved house
when I was about 10 or 11 years old.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.360
Being carers for my parents

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.640
we got a bit more
of a hands-on experience

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.960
of the whole property move.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.760
So I tried to understand
the break-down of costs

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.440
and a big one inevitably
was estate agency fees.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:36.680
So a few years later

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.520
we learnt about different disruptors
in different industries,

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.800
taxi driving for Uber,

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.200
shopping with Amazon,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.280
this is where I thought, "actually,
why don't we try and connect

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.640
buyers and sellers directly?"

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.640
My next tip is...

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.400
For me that was
Michael O'Leary's biography

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.920
about when he started Ryanair.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.080
A good biography
is really, really motivating.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.000
It shows you that it's doable,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.840
it shows you that actually
the people that started

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.160
these insanely successful
businesses were normal people

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.080
and they've decided to step out of
their comfort zone and take a risk,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.640
and that's really inspiring.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.560
...new things, is my next top tip.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.760
I think without teaching yourself,
it's difficult to start a business,

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.680
because you need
to understand every aspect

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.200
and whether it's your forte or not,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.880
and understanding that means
you can test the business cheaply.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.600
And for me that was learning
floor plans from scratch,

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:29.680
photography from scratch,
things I didn't have experience in.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.840
So one thing I couldn't
learn myself unfortunately

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.600
at that point
was learning to drive.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.160
That's because I was
only about 16 or 17,

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.680
so to get to my first property

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.200
I did have to pay
my sister's boyfriend to drive me

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.600
down to the property in East Sussex
so I could then go and do the visit.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:47.400
My next tip is...

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.640
I've understood that my Mum

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.080
95% of the time is right,
and she is wiser than yourself.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.080
So my Mum has been very influential,
well both my parents have.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.880
They've never let their disability
stand in their way.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:10.080
Seeing that work ethic especially
tracing back to migration for example

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.800
for my Dad, from Africa into the UK.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.320
It's not easy,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.200
and they've actually managed
to get our family to the stage

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.280
where we are now

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.600
where I'm actually able
to take a risk in my own life

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.320
because I know I've got
this stable family background.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.560
To be honest
the success and the money

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.160
hasn't made a difference to my life.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.440
I do still live at home,
still live with my parents.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.040
I still go to the same type
of places that I used to,

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.160
I still don't have a car.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.360
It's difficult to actually
take a step back

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.800
and look at the progress
you've made

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.600
because I'm always kind of
moving the goalposts further away.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.720
So at the moment we're a start-up,
we're in the midst of growing,

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.560
it's really, really exciting stages,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.320
but I think we've got
so much more to give.

